+# ADR 0120: StandardLit PBR Textures and Generated Tangents
+
+- Status: Accepted
+- Date: 2026-08-15
+
+## Context
+
+ADR 0119 established a generic tangent GPU stream and one authoritative
+material fragment stage for static and skinned meshes, but StandardLit still
+used scalar roughness/metallic values and ignored imported occlusion maps.
+Normal maps also had no authored scale, and meshes without source tangents
+always relied on derivative reconstruction.
+
+glTF already exposes the metallic-roughness, occlusion, and normal-scale
+semantics needed by the generic material model. These concepts are not glTF
+renderer features: FBX, procedural assets, editor-authored materials, and
+future formats need the same engine-level representation.
+
+ADR 0115 requires persisted formats to have one current schema rather than
+accumulating compatibility readers.
+
+## Decision
+
+### Material schema v3
+
+`.material.json` schema version 3 is the only accepted material document
+version. Version 2 is rejected explicitly and repository-authored fixtures and
+writers move to v3 in the same change.
+
+The generic material contract adds:
+
+- `metallic_roughness_texture`: optional linear-data texture,
+- `occlusion_texture`: optional linear-data texture,
+- `normal_scale`: finite scalar, default `1.0`,
+- `occlusion_strength`: scalar in `[0, 1]`, default `1.0`.
+
+Existing `roughness` and `metallic` remain scalar factors.
+
+For StandardLit, the packed metallic-roughness texture follows the common
+channel contract: green multiplies roughness and blue multiplies metallic.
+The occlusion texture uses red. A missing numeric texture behaves as white,
+so scalar-only materials are unchanged.
+
+`normal_scale` multiplies tangent-space X/Y before the sampled normal is
+normalized. It is allowed to be negative; only non-finite values are invalid.
+
+Per ADR 0118, normal, metallic-roughness, and occlusion textures are linear
+numeric data. Base color, emissive, toon ramp, and sphere/matcap textures keep
+their color-texture semantics.
+
+Ambient occlusion modulates ambient/indirect lighting only. It does not darken
+the direct directional-light term. `occlusion_strength` blends between no
+occlusion and the sampled red channel.
+
+ToonLit and Unlit keep their independent shading contracts and do not acquire
+StandardLit metallic-roughness or AO behavior.
+
+### Import normalization
+
+The format-independent model IR carries the four new generic material
+semantics. glTF maps its native packed metallic-roughness texture, occlusion
+texture/strength, and normal scale directly into that IR. Formats without a
+native equivalent emit the generic defaults. Renderer code never branches on
+the source format.
+
+Extracted imported materials use the same schema-v3 `MaterialAsset` contract
+as standalone author-authored materials, preserving ADR 0101's remap model.
+
+### Tangent generation
+
+Source tangents always win.
+
+When a mesh has no source tangents and at least one material using it has a
+normal map, the import builder runs the MikkTSpace reference algorithm before
+the mesh crosses into runtime rendering. The dependency is isolated to
+`engine-import`; renderer/runtime crates do not depend on MikkTSpace.
+
+MikkTSpace returns per-face-corner tangent frames. The engine does not silently
+split imported topology because doing so would require synchronized remapping
+of skinning, morphs, and other per-vertex data. A generated tangent is therefore
+accepted into the existing per-vertex stream only when all corners sharing
+that vertex agree on tangent direction and handedness. If generation fails or
+shared corners require incompatible frames, the mesh keeps no generated
+tangent stream and the renderer retains ADR 0119's derivative TBN fallback.
+The importer emits a diagnostic for that fallback instead of producing an
+incorrect indexed tangent basis.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- StandardLit can represent the core metallic-roughness texture contract of
+  glTF without a format-specific render path.
+- Numeric texture color space is explicit end-to-end.
+- Existing scalar-only materials preserve their appearance through white
+  fallback data textures.
+- Normal mapping quality improves automatically for suitable meshes without
+  changing the public `Vertex` layout or serialized mesh identity.
+- Some meshes with UV seams encoded through shared indices still use the
+  derivative fallback until a future topology-remapping import stage can split
+  vertices together with every dependent per-vertex channel.
+- Material schema v2 assets must be regenerated or rewritten as v3; no legacy
+  parser is retained.
+- Outline, shadow, Stable ID, and crate ownership contracts are unchanged.
